    objective: 'Compare the installed scoring rate with the chosen role over its exact clock window. Freeze the trial settings and include a held-out case when refining the design. Test whether two supports can carry the weight while the robot still rotates about the line joining them. Trace every path through acceleration, hold and power loss. Record the exact candidate, inputs, observed result and limitations.',
    activity: 'Locate each installed mount against its drawing datums. If two CAD measurements disagree about whether solids share material, probe both at the same installed coordinates before deciding which part needs correction. When the STEP intersection has invalid geometry, open the two named native bodies and inspect the contact at the same pose. If identical installed fits give different volumes at different robot corners, inspect the named pair at each corner and compare the native measurements. Follow a piece from its donor onto the commissioned receiving support. Measure the load the receiver carries and the time the whole piece remains inside its intended region. Check the departing support for remaining contact. Check where every robot part travels as the mechanism extends, including at the scoring pose. A sensor may see the piece enter the goal while a slide strikes the field. Trace installed loads through structural hardware into the chassis. Test a compression-only seat against simultaneous uplift, side load and tipping before counting on a bracket to hold it down. A nearby cover can hide a gap underneath a bracket. Can the bolted parts pull through the frame together? Move the complete stack in the load direction, identify its stop and check the load that stop can carry. Compare the recorded path with the full robot and field inventories. Replay the same loaded trip against the other five robots on the match clock; locate the first occupied lane and trace which later handoffs are still reachable. Then shift the target map toward the tightest clearance. Compare the observer decision with the margin in an independent field frame.',
    handoff: 'Review the detailed design on the saved, reopened Fusion file in five steps: part identity, interference and attachment, shop and service, loads, then the verdict. A build-ready outcome requires passing the applicable installed checks. Compare every installed body, test the service and operating paths, and show the governing load and repeated trials. Every published design holds a status: in-progress, effective, superseded or withdrawn. A preliminary design reads as its name, version and status, such as “Tilting carriage 1.0 · effective”. Drafts do not consume numbers: an edit to an in-progress design is staged again under the same version as the next draft, and a new number is only for a new effective baseline or a new architecture. A detailed design takes the same name as its preliminary design and its own version: the major number is the architecture, the minor number counts the detailed effective baselines, and each publish of an in-progress version is a draft, as in “Tilting carriage 1.1 · draft 3 · in progress”. When the next preliminary baseline is accepted, the in-progress detailed design is re-linked to it and keeps its version. A detailed design can publish as in-progress once it links the effective preliminary design, its Fusion export passes the layout check and a mission animation plays. Each design has one viewer, its design review page, in progress and effective: the README still and the results page link to it and embed no model of their own. The bench tests the preliminary design hands over become records in detailed design, each tagged before-build or on-robot with an owner and a plan. It becomes effective, and takes the primary view, in one reviewed pull request that carries its passing mechanical review, a recorded passing result for every before-build bench test and an owner and a plan for every on-robot one; a review that does not pass blocks the change. The review rests on the recorded evidence, with no key or signature to hold and no hash to type: its study names each file by path. The Fusion archive is committed in the project and moves between computers with Git. A design is withdrawn in one pull request too: the index keeps the reason and the last commit, and the files leave the site. The project’s CI checks every pushed branch with one command. It reruns the layout check once, when a design becomes effective, and never for a draft, a rename or a view refresh. It refuses a change to the physics of an effective design: that work is the next minor version, opened as a draft. Every command says what it ran, what it did not and what CI will do on push. The project’s workflow file and its stage.py are a few lines that call the plugin at its current release, and project setup writes a Design checks block into the project’s AGENTS.md that says what to run for which change. A preliminary design release stays a secondary page, in progress or effective.',
